# Individual Work Report

For my part of the project, I worked on the Reservation and the linked list for our Campus Resource Reservation System. My main job was to create the reservation information and make a linked list to store the active reservations. I also worked on testing my code, compiling it in VS Code, and using GitHub to save and manage my work.

First, I worked on the Reservation class. I created the `Reservation` files, `Reservation.h` and `Reservation.cpp`. The reservation stores the important information about a reservation, such as the reservation ID, student ID, resource ID, and timestamp. I made the Reservation object so that each reservation can hold its own information.

After creating the Reservation, I worked on the linked list. I used a `ReservationNode` to hold a reservation and a pointer to the next reservation. I used a `head` pointer to keep track of the first reservation in the linked list. When a new reservation is created, it can be added to the list. The node points to the next node, so the reservations are connected together.

I worked on the main linked list operations in `ReservationManager`. I worked on inserting a reservation, searching and validating a reservation, removing a reservation, and displaying the reservations. For removing a reservation, I had to make sure the links were changed correctly so the rest of the list would still work. I also worked on the destructor so the dynamically created nodes could be cleaned up.

One thing I learned from this part is how a linked list is different from an array. With the linked list, each node has the reservation information and a pointer to the next node. I had to understand the `head` and the `next` pointer because if the links are wrong, the program can lose part of the list or crash.

I also looked at the time complexity of my operations. Inserting at the head can be O(1) because I don't have to go through the whole list. Searching, validating, removing, and displaying can be O(n) because the program may need to go through the reservations one by one. The space used by the linked list is O(n) because every reservation needs a node.

For my coding work, I used VS Code. I opened the project in VS Code and worked with the C++ files. I compiled the program and ran it from the VS Code terminal. When I got errors, I went back to the code and fixed them. I tested the reservation and linked list functions to make sure the reservations were being added, found, displayed, and removed correctly.

I also used GitHub to manage my work. I created and worked on a feature branch instead of making all my changes directly on the main branch. After making my changes, I checked the files with Git, added my changes, and made commits describing what I worked on. I pushed my branch to GitHub and used a pull request to merge my work into the main branch. This helped me understand how branches and commits are used when working on a group project.

Overall, my main contribution to the project was the Reservation and active reservation linked list. I learned how to create a class, connect objects using nodes and pointers, insert and remove nodes, search through a linked list, and think about the time complexity of each operation. I also got more practice compiling C++ in VS Code and using GitHub branches, commits, and pull requests to manage my work.
